      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Rotary screw compressors use oil-flooded lubrication in which oil is injected directly into the compression chamber to seal rotor clearances, cool the compression process, and lubricate the rotor bearings. The oil must have excellent oxidation stability and a low tendency to form deposits, because it is continuously recirculated at elevated temperatures (typically 80–100°C at the oil separator outlet). Mineral paraffinic Group II oils are standard for conventional 2000–4000 hour drain intervals; PAO-based synthetic compressor oils extend drain intervals to 6000–8000 hours or beyond and are recommended where high ambient temperatures, continuous duty cycles, or reduced maintenance frequency are required. ISO VG 46 is the most widely used viscosity grade for rotary screw air compressors in temperate climates; ISO VG 68 is selected for high-ambient or high-pressure applications." }
