      <p class="img-caption">Polyol ester (POE) refrigeration lubricants are characteristically clear and pale golden — their ester chemistry provides full miscibility with HFC refrigerants but makes moisture contamination a critical handling risk throughout storage and service.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="gas-compressor">
        <h2>Gas Compressor Oil Formulation: Natural Gas, CO₂, and Process Gases</h2>
        <p>
          Gas compressors handling hydrocarbons, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, or process gases present lubrication challenges that are absent from air compression service. The compressed gas itself interacts directly with the lubricant — it may dissolve in the oil, stripping viscosity and disrupting the hydrodynamic film; it may chemically react with base fluid or additives; or it may be contaminated by lubricant carryover that is unacceptable in the downstream process. Each gas type imposes specific lubricant compatibility requirements that override general compressor oil selection criteria.
        </p>
        <p>
          Natural gas pipeline compressors — both reciprocating and rotary screw — face gas stripping as the dominant lubrication challenge. Light hydrocarbon components (methane, ethane) dissolve readily in paraffinic mineral oil and PAO, temporarily reducing the effective viscosity of the oil in the compression zone and reducing the film thickness at bearing surfaces. Lubricant selection must account for the solubility of the specific gas composition in the candidate oil, and viscosity grades are often selected one or two grades heavier than the equivalent air-service recommendation to compensate for viscosity reduction by dissolved gas. For CO₂ compressor service — including industrial CO₂ compression and transcritical CO₂ refrigeration systems — naphthenic mineral oils and certain POE grades are used; PAO has limited solubility with supercritical CO₂ and may separate under high-pressure transcritical conditions. Hydrogen compressors present a different challenge: hydrogen has exceptionally low molecular weight and high diffusivity, and dissolves very little in lubricating oil — viscosity stripping is minimal — but hydrogen service requires lubricants with very low vapour pressure and good film strength, as PAO and PFPE-based lubricants are the standard grades for high-pressure hydrogen compression. Oxygen compressor lubrication is the most restrictive of all — oxygen is a powerful oxidiser that makes most organic lubricants potential fire or explosion hazards; PFPE (perfluoropolyether) lubricants, which are chemically inert to oxygen even at high pressure and temperature, are mandatory for oxygen compression service, as described in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfluoropolyether" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">overview of PFPE lubricant chemistry</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Never use a standard mineral or PAO compressor oil in an oxygen compressor, even temporarily. Organic lubricants in contact with high-pressure oxygen can auto-ignite or detonate at temperatures far below their measured flash point — the flash point test, which measures ignition in air, dramatically underestimates the ignition hazard in a pressurised oxygen atmosphere. Only certified PFPE lubricants approved for oxygen service may be used.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Infographic image -->
      <img 
        class="article-img" 
        src={`https://cdn.globalformulation.com/images/${slug}/${slug}-infographic-globalformulation.webp`} 
        alt="air compressor oil specification chart — test tubes showing compressor oil samples from mineral to PAO synthetic to POE ester grades | Global Formulation infographic" 
        width="900" 
        height="500" 
        loading="lazy" 
      />
      <p class="img-caption">Compressor lubricants range from pale straw mineral oils to golden PAO synthetics to near-water-clear POE esters — appearance alone does not reveal base chemistry, viscosity grade, or refrigerant compatibility, making lubricant identification labels and product datasheets the only reliable selection guide.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="synthetic-grades">
        <h2>Synthetic Compressor Oil Selection: PAO, POE, and PAG</h2>
        <p>
          The decision to specify a synthetic compressor oil over a mineral alternative involves an economic calculation that must account for extended drain intervals, reduced oil consumption, lower energy consumption (for low-viscosity synthetics at low temperatures), and reduced maintenance frequency — not simply the higher unit cost of the synthetic. In high-utilisation rotary screw compressors running continuous 24-hour duty cycles, a PAO synthetic oil at three times the mineral oil price may deliver a lower total cost of ownership when the value of reduced oil change frequency, lower oil consumption from reduced carryover, and avoidance of deposit-related service calls is factored into the calculation.
        </p>
        <p>
          PAO-based synthetic compressor oils dominate the air compressor synthetic market. Their branched saturated hydrocarbon structure provides excellent oxidation stability — critical for extended drain intervals — a naturally high viscosity index (reducing viscosity variation with temperature), low pour point (down to −60°C for some grades), and good compatibility with the mineral-based elastomers and seals used in most compressor designs. Unlike PAG, PAO is compatible with mineral oil and can be mixed for top-up without system damage. POE lubricants are the mandatory choice for hermetic HFC refrigeration compressors — their ester chemistry provides the refrigerant miscibility that no other lubricant class achieves with HFCs. The hygroscopic nature of POE demands strict moisture-exclusion procedures throughout handling, storage, and servicing — failure to control moisture leads to acid generation and accelerated bearing wear that presents as an unexplained early-life failure. PAG compressor oils occupy a specialist niche: they are used in some ammonia refrigeration systems, specific automotive air conditioning applications with R-134a, and certain industrial process gas applications where their very high natural lubricity is advantageous. PAG is incompatible with mineral oil and PAO — a full system flush is required before conversion. The additive requirements for compressor oils are covered in depth in our guide to <a href="/additives-in-lubricants-guide" target="_top">additives in lubricants</a>, which addresses antioxidants, corrosion inhibitors, antifoam, and demulsifiers across all lubricant categories. For a comprehensive overview of synthetic and mineral base fluid performance in demanding applications, our resource on <a href="/lubricant-formulations-and-technology" target="_top">lubricant formulations and technology</a> provides the full technical framework.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What type of oil is used in rotary screw compressors?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Rotary screw compressors use oil-flooded lubrication in which oil is injected directly into the compression chamber to seal rotor clearances, cool compression, and lubricate bearings. The oil is continuously recirculated at 80–100°C and must have excellent oxidation stability and low foaming tendency. Mineral Group II oils are standard for 2000–4000 hour drain intervals; PAO synthetics extend service to 6000–8000+ hours. ISO VG 46 is the most widely specified grade for temperate-climate operation; ISO VG 68 is used for high-ambient or high-pressure applications. Always follow OEM viscosity grade recommendations — deviating voids most equipment warranties.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why do HFC refrigerant systems require polyol ester (POE) oil?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">HFC refrigerants (R-134a, R-410A, R-32, R-404A) are not miscible with mineral oil or alkylbenzene lubricants. Miscibility across the full circuit temperature range — including at evaporator temperatures of −30°C to −50°C — is mandatory to prevent oil pooling in the evaporator, which reduces heat transfer and blocks expansion valves. POE lubricants are fully miscible with all HFCs. POE oils are hygroscopic: moisture absorption causes ester hydrolysis, generating acids that corrode compressor bearings and cause copper plating. POE must be stored in sealed containers and systems must be thoroughly dried before filling.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between oil-flooded and oil-free compressors?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Oil-flooded compressors inject lubricant directly into the compression chamber — oil seals rotor clearances, cools compression, and lubricates internal surfaces, but leaves residual oil carryover (1–5 mg/m³) in the compressed air, removed by a downstream coalescing separator. Oil-free compressors use precision-machined clearances or PTFE-coated surfaces to compress gas without oil contact in the compression path — the output is genuinely oil-free, which is mandatory for pharmaceutical, food processing, and electronics manufacturing. Oil-free compressors still require external bearing and gearbox lubrication but exclude lubricant from the compressed gas stream.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can I mix synthetic and mineral compressor oil?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">PAO and mineral oil are compatible for mixing — blending occurs without phase separation, though a full drain and refill with PAO synthetic is preferred to realise the full extended-drain benefit. PAG is NOT compatible with mineral oil or PAO: mixing causes phase separation and sludge formation that can block oil passages and cause compressor damage. A full system drain and flush with a dedicated flushing fluid is required before converting to PAG. POE is generally compatible with mineral oil for mixing but blending dilutes HFC miscibility and oxidation stability — not recommended for refrigeration applications.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What causes carbon deposit formation in reciprocating air compressors?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Carbon deposits in reciprocating compressor valve areas result from thermal oxidation of lubricant contacting compressed air at discharge temperatures of 150–200°C. Dissolved oxygen attacks unsaturated bonds in the lubricant, generating peroxides that polymerise into varnish and then hard carbon. Accelerating factors include excessive discharge temperature, over-oiling of cylinder walls, mineral Group I oil (high aromatic content), extended drain intervals, and moisture contamination. PAO and diester synthetic oils deposit far less carbon than mineral oils at equivalent operating conditions due to their saturated molecular structure and minimal aromatic content.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What viscosity grade should I use for an air compressor?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">ISO VG 46 is the standard for oil-flooded rotary screw air compressors in temperate climates (15–35°C ambient). ISO VG 68 is used for high-ambient temperatures (above 35°C), high-pressure single-stage designs, or continuous high-duty-cycle operation. Reciprocating compressor cylinders typically require ISO VG 100–150 due to higher discharge temperatures and longer oil-metal contact times. Always verify the OEM specification: manufacturers determine the correct grade based on rotor clearance, bearing design, and thermal loading, and deviating from the specified grade voids most equipment warranties and can cause premature bearing wear or oil carryover problems.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

